{
  "image_name": "1d7ddad05aeb9705a48c7992500c2d38.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how blocking protein sumoylation can reshape immune-cell gene programs and reduce autoimmunity. Across the panels, sumoylated transcriptional regulators are shown as repressors on DNA, and removing or inhibiting that modification allows key immune genes to be expressed.\n\nOne mechanism involves NFATc1 at the IL2 locus in T cells. In its sumoylated state, NFATc1 appears to suppress IL2, whereas loss of sumoylation permits IL2 expression, promoting regulatory T-cell expansion and inhibition of IL-17 responses, which is linked to reduced autoimmunity.\n\nAnother mechanism is shown in myeloid cells, where a SUMO-associated complex involving SetDB1 seems to maintain silencing of endogenous retroviral elements and restrain MHC-I expression. Inhibiting sumoylation lifts this repression, leading to ERV activation, interferon signaling, and increased antigen-presentation machinery.\n\nThe right side focuses on T-cell subsets. In CD4+ T cells, sumoylated MAF, together with cofactors such as DAXX and HDAC2 and sequestration in PML bodies, appears to block IL21 and IL4 expression. When this repression is relieved, IL-4 and IL-21 are produced. These cytokines then act on CD8+ T cells, enhancing genes associated with effector function, including CD44 and granzyme B. Overall, the figure links sumoylation inhibition to improved immune regulation and protection from type 1 diabetes.",
